<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cristero War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1926">1926</a>–<a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cristero War (1926–1929) was an armed uprising in central and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> against the anticlerical laws of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarco-elias-calles">Plutarco Elías Calles</a>. Catholic rebels, the Cristeros, fought federal troops chiefly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a> and neighbouring states, and tens of thousands died. It ended with an agreement between the government and the Church brokered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> ambassador Dwight Morrow.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cristero-war.jpg" alt="Cristero War" width="320" height="359" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zbitka_sze%C5%9Bciu_ilustracji_z_Powstania_Cristero_1926-1929.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a> began in 1910 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porfirio-diaz">Porfirio Díaz</a>. Under Díaz the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and the state had an informal modus vivendi in which the anticlerical articles of the liberal Constitution of 1857 were kept on paper but not enforced. The Constitution of 1917, drawn up under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venustiano-carranza">Venustiano Carranza</a>, strengthened them: Article 3 made education secular, Article 27 limited the property religious associations could hold, and Article 130 separated the state from the churches. Carranza and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvaro-obregon">Álvaro Obregón</a> were lenient in enforcing these articles, especially where the Church was powerful.</p><p>The rebellion was a response to President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plutarco-elias-calles">Plutarco Elías Calles</a>'s decree to enforce Article 130 strictly, an act known as the Calles Law. In July 1926 Catholic bishops endorsed an economic boycott, particularly effective in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aguascalientes">Aguascalientes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a>. In September the Mexican Congress rejected the bishops' proposal to amend the offending articles, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-xi">Pope Pius XI</a> denounced the persecution in the encyclical Iniquis afflictisque on 18 November 1926.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The government called the rebels Cristeros because they invoked Cristo Rey, Christ the King, and they soon took the name themselves. On 3 August 1926, in Guadalajara, some 400 armed Catholics shut themselves in a church and surrendered when their ammunition ran out; American consular sources reported 18 dead and 40 wounded. Further uprisings followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a> and Durango. Their leaders mostly used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics, since their forces were no match for federal troops, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a> became the main focus. The Feminine Brigades of St. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a> helped smuggle guns and ammunition.</p><p>In October 1927 the American ambassador Dwight Morrow began a series of breakfast meetings with Calles, aided by Father John J. Burke of the National Catholic Welfare Conference. The assassination of the president-elect Obregón by the Catholic radical José de León Toral damaged the peace process. Emilio Portes Gil, interim president from September 1928, allowed the talks to resume, and in May 1929 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> said they would not demand repeal of the laws, only more lenient enforcement. The conflict ended on 21 June 1929.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The government often did not keep the terms of the truce; it executed some 500 Cristero leaders and 5,000 other Cristeros. Between 1926 and 1934 at least 40 priests were killed. Of 4,500 priests serving before the rebellion, only 334 were licensed to serve 15 million Catholics by 1934. A second wave of armed resistance in the late 1930s, partly against socialist education, is called the Second Cristero War. Historians interpret the rebellion as a stage in the church-state struggle that reaches back to the War of Reform, and as the last major peasant uprising after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cristero War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The United States occupied Veracruz in 1914 and sent the Punitive Expedition after Villa in 1916, and the <b>Cristero War</b> of 1926–1929 pitted Catholic rebels against the state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Military</a></li></ul></section>
